Coastal wetlands, such as salt marshes and mangroves, act as natural buffers that significantly mitigate storm surge and wave energy before they reach developed shorelines. This protective capacity relies on three primary physical mechanisms. First, vegetation stems and leaves create surface roughness, generating drag that slows water flow and dissipates wave energy through friction. Second, the complex root systems and dense vegetation trap sediment, gradually raising the elevation of the wetland floor. This elevation increase reduces the depth of water flowing over the land, further decreasing the volume and velocity of floodwaters. Third, wetlands act as a sponge; their porous soils absorb